  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Federal contract obligations to Indiana total $3.1B for FY2026 to date. Award volume is 14,414 awards with an average award size of $215K. Obligations are down 55.8% year over year. Historical totals show swings: FY21 was $6.3B, FY22 was $8.2B, FY23 was $6B, FY24 was $5.3B, and FY25 was $7B. The Department of Defense accounts for the largest share of FY2026 to date buying at $2.2B, followed by the Department of Homeland Security at $235M and the Department of Health and Human Services at $176M. Top vendors include ROLLS-ROYCE CORPORATION at $749M and UNDERSEA SENSOR SYSTEMS, INC. at $243M. Major industry concentrations are Aircraft Engine and Engine Parts Manufacturing at $711M and Search, Detection, Navigation, Guidance, Aeronautical, and Nautical System and Instrument Manufacturing at $333M. All figures are contract obligations; current-year totals are partial (fiscal year to date). This snapshot shows a lower current-year total compared with several recent fiscal years and highlights defense and aircraft-related spending as dominant components of the current-year picture.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "What drove the year-over-year change in obligations?",
        "body": "Obligations are down 55.8% year over year. The Department of Defense remains the largest buyer at $2.2B, but total FY2026 to date obligations are $3.1B, lower than FY25 at $7B and FY22 at $8.2B. Large vendor concentrations such as ROLLS-ROYCE CORPORATION at $749M and industry clusters like Aircraft Engine and Engine Parts Manufacturing at $711M mean shifts in a few big programs can move the total sharply. The combination of high concentration and a partial fiscal year to date explains much of the current decline in obligations."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which agencies and industries are the largest parts of the market now?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense is the dominant buyer at $2.2B, followed by the Department of Homeland Security at $235M and the Department of Health and Human Services at $176M. Top industries are Aircraft Engine and Engine Parts Manufacturing at $711M and Search, Detection, Navigation, Guidance, Aeronautical, and Nautical System and Instrument Manufacturing at $333M. Top vendors include ROLLS-ROYCE CORPORATION at $749M and UNDERSEA SENSOR SYSTEMS, INC. at $243M, which together represent large shares of the FY2026 to date obligations. The pattern shows defense and aerospace-related dollars concentrated among a few suppliers and NAICS categories."
      }
    ]
  },
  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "How much federal contract money goes to Indiana?",
      "a": "Indiana has $3.1B in federal contract obligations in FY2026 to date. Total award volume is 14,414 and the average award size is $215K. This current-year total is partial and obligations are down 55.8% year over year. Historical fiscal year totals were $6.3B in FY21, $8.2B in FY22, $6B in FY23, $5.3B in FY24, and $7B in FY25."
    },
    {
      "q": "Which agencies award the most contracts in Indiana?",
      "a": "The Department of Defense awards the most contracts in Indiana at $2.2B in FY2026 to date. The next largest buying agencies are the Department of Homeland Security at $235M and the Department of Health and Human Services at $176M. Other notable buyers include the Department of Veterans Affairs at $153M and the General Services Administration at $72M."
    },
